  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Heat and humidity continues tomorrow. Break in the \nheat late in the weekend, but heat builds again towards the middle \nof next week.\n\nMuch of the southeastern US and northward into the Mid Atlantic are \nunder the influence of a strong 500mb ridge, allowing the heat and \nhumidity to continue today and through tomorrow. High temperatures \nwill reach the mid to upper 90s in the Piedmont today, potentially \nreaching 100 degrees for some locations, which is 10 to 17 degrees \nabove seasonal normals. Heat indices will range from  100 to 105 \ntoday, though any cloud cover and precipitation could prevent \nreaching the upper end of that range. A Heat Advisory remains in \neffect for the VA and NC Piedmont through this evening, and one may \nbe considered for tomorrow as well, but with some uncertainty in \nhigh temps given the possibility for storms and cloud cover, \nconfidence is not as high in reaching advisory criteria at this time.\n\nFollowing a frontal passage Saturday, Sunday and Monday will be \ncooler, with high temperatures in the mid 70s in the west and low to \nmid 80s in the east, only a couple degrees above normal. Guidance is \nstarting to indicate a wedge pattern setting up over the region, \nwhich typically keeps the area cooler and cloudy, though depends how \nfar south the front moves. Could see temps for Sunday trend a degree \nor two lower in subsequent forecasts. \n\nBy the start of the next work week, a 500mb ridge over the \ncentral US begins to get suppressed by a trough tracking from \nthe Pacific NW towards the northern Plains. The ridge will \nbecome more centered over the southern and eastern US, though \nsome models show some degree of troughing lingering over the \nnortheast. Nonetheless, rising heights underneath the expanding \nridge will lead to increasing temperatures, pushing above normal\nonce again, for the middle part of the week. NBM probabilities \nfor max temperatures 90 degrees or higher are 50% to 75% for \nparts of the VA and NC Piedmont Wednesday, and 70% to 80% by \nThursday.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Strong to severe thunderstorms today, and especially \nFriday and Saturday.\n\nThe hot and humid airmass settled over the region today will \ncontribute to plenty of instability by the afternoon, with SBCAPE \nreaching as high as 1500 J/kg or so. However, the stronger bulk \nshear is farther east and north, in central and northern VA. Some \nCAMs are showing storms developing along the southern Blue Ridge \nlater this afternoon and evening, driven by some low level \nconvergence along the higher terrain. Overall, confidence is lower \nin storm intensity and coverage today, than tomorrow (Friday). A \ncold front drops south into the area by Friday evening, which will \nprovide better forcing for ascent for convection. An upper shortwave \nwill move into the area Saturday, which again gives additional \nsupport for thunderstorm development. Damaging wind gusts are \nthe most likely threat, with isolated hail and heavy rain also \npossible in storms today and the next few days.\n\nModels have trended the movement of the front slower, so Sunday\nmay also be a wet day, though most of the showers and storms \nlook to be over the mountains and Foothills, along the frontal \nboundary.",
